== Politics and government ==
{{Main|Politics of Skade}}

[[File:Althing building Asøn.jpg|thumb|The [[Althing (Skade)|Althing]] building in [[Asøn]]]]

According to the [[Constitution of Skade]], which was adopted on 17 May 2013, Skade is a [[Unitary state|unitary]] [[Constitutional monarchy|constitutional monarchy]] with a [[Parliamentary system|parliamentary system of government]], wherein the [[Monarchy of Skade|King of Skade]] is the [[head of state]] and the [[Prime Minister of Skade|Prime Minister]] is the [[head of government]]. Power is separated among the [[Legislature|legislative]], [[Executive (government)|executive]] and [[Judiciary|judicial]] branches of government, as defined by the Constitution, which serves as the country's supreme legal document.

It outlines basic and political rights of individuals and citizen participation in public affairs, divides the powers between the government and the [[Cantons of Skade|cantons]] and defines the national government's jurisdiction and authority. There are three main governing bodies on the national level: the [[Althing (Skade)|Althing]] (legislative), the [[Council of State (Skade)|Council of State]] (executive) and the [[National Court of Skade]] (judicial). The current [[Prime Minister of Skade|Prime Minister]] of Skade is [[Gissel Adelsköld]], the leader of the [[Citizens' Party (Skade)|Citizens' Party]] (pictured right). The next elections are scheduled for 17 February 2017.

The Monarch is the head of state, with strictly representative and ceremonial roles, such as formal appointment and dismissal of the Prime Minister, the [[Commander-in-Chief]] of the [[Forsvarsmakt|Armed Forces]], and a symbol of unity for Skadians. The monarch is elective (although essentially hereditary, as the only candidates come from the [[House of Kjellfrid]]), and they are chosen by the 17 members of the Council of State, and serves for life (or until [[Abdication|abdication]]). The current Monarch is [[Alexander X of Skade|Alexander X]], who has reigned since 21 February 2009.

The Althing is the parliament of Skade, which consists of 153 members who are elected under a system of [[Proportional representation|proportional representation]] from the 17 cantons, and it also serves to elect the Prime Minister of Skade, although effectively the Prime Minister is the leader of the largest party. Members of the Althing serve for 5 years, and have no term limits. The Council of State is made up of 18 senior members of government chosen by the Prime Minister, and functions as the collective decision-making organ constituting the executive branch of the Kingdom. Through referendums, citizens may challenge any law passed by parliament and through initiatives, introduce amendments to the federal constitution, thus establishing a [[Direct democracy|direct democracy]].

=== Judiciary system and law ===
{{Main|Judiciary of Skade|Law of Skade}}

Skade uses a [[Civil law (legal system)|civil law system]] where laws are created and amended in Parliament and the system regulated through the [[National Court of Skade]]. The judiciary is independent of executive and legislative branches. While the Prime Minister nominates National Court Justices for office, their nomination must be approved by Parliament and formally confirmed by the Monarch in the Council of State. Usually, judges attached to regular courts are formally appointed by the Monarch on the advice of the Prime Minister.

Skade abolished the [[Capital punishment|death penalty]] for regular criminal acts in 1985, although before then there was a mandatory death penalty for murder, as well as for certain aggravated drug-trafficking and firearms offences. The Althing later abolished the death penalty for [[W:High treason|high treason]] in war and [[W:War crimes|war-crimes]] in 1991. The Skadian penal system now focuses on criminal prevention and [[Rehabilitation (penology)|rehabilitation]].

As part of the '''''[[Stortåpning]]''''' reforms ({{lang|sk|"the great opening"}}), the Althing removed provisions from the Constitution which required civilians to pledge to uphold the "Skadian race" (thus legalizing [[Miscegenation|miscegenation]]), while easing restrictions on external immigration, repealing laws which disenfranchised existing minorities, significantly scaling back the power of the [[SIOS]], and greatly increasing government transparency. However Skade is still criticized by some as an [[Illiberal democracy|illiberal democracy]], citing a continued policy of detention without trial, suppression of speech deemed harmful to Skadian unity, and excessive [[Whitewashing (censorship)|whitewashing]] of matters deemed sensitive or damaging to Skade's social cohesion.

Skadian law in regards to religion are considered to be contradictory. The '''''[[Separasjonlov]]''''' ({{lang|sk|"Separation law"}}) removed the right of religious organizations to interfere in political matters, and withdrew recognition of religious beliefs as a legitimate reason for the proposal or passage of laws and treatment of an individual on Skadian soil. However the state maintained [[Ásatrú]]'s status as the official religion of Skade, while the state gained control over the leadership of the state church.

Skade's relationship with the [[Freedom of religion|freedom of religion]] is complex. The Parliament has listed many religious movements as dangerous [[Cult|cults]] since 1995, and has banned wearing conspicuous religious symbols in schools since 1954 (with the one exception being Røhnisch Ásatrú). In 1960, it banned the wearing of face-covering Islamic veils in public (despite the Muslim population making up less than 0.6% of the population). Adherents to minority religions have complained that they suffer from discrimination in housing, education, and the workplace due to Skadian law. While these laws are controversial internationally, they are supported by the majority.

=== Direct democracy and elections ===
{{Main|Elections in Skade|Direct democracy in Skade}}

Skade's form of [[W:Direct democracy|direct democracy]] (''direkte demokrati'') is often called "[[Half-direct democracy|half direct]]", as the system includes institutions of the typical representative democracy, used to support the main system. Direct-democracy has long been present in Skade, being used by the [[Asgeir]] to select their kings, establish basic laws, and decision making. This process, called '''''[[folketsvalg]]''''' ({{lang|sk|"people's choice"}}) was continued throughout Skade (and the other South Illypnian states) up until the [[Skadian Civil War]] and the rise of the [[Rådskåde]] regime, in which power was vested wholly in the [[Skadian Council (Skade)|Skadian Council]]. It was only restored with the adoption of the Constitution of 1985. Between 2000 and 2015 alone Skadians voted 215 times on 59 different political issues, the highest number in the world. Public meetings and debates are commonly used to discuss political issues, and to allow citizens to voice their issues, concerns, or ideas.

The [[Voting age|voting age]] in Skade is 16, as established by a public referendum approved by 56% of voters in 1999, in line with the Ásatrú tradition of age 16 being considered the [[Age of majority|age of majority]]. Voting was mandatory in the country until 2007, and those that did not vote were penalized with a {{currency symbol|sku|Ꭶ}}5 fine. The law was repealed by popular vote, with 75% in favor. Skade still has some of the highest [[Voter turnout|voter turnout]] in the world, averaging above 80% consistently since the 1990s.

Once a person settles in Skade, they are required by law to register with the municipality they reside in within a week, so the local government will know their address, thus eliminating the need for citizens to register to vote themselves. In the weeks before a referendum, or an election, ballot cards are sent out in sealed envelopes to every citizen, who casts their vote, signs a card to identify themselves, and sends it back to the municipality. The transmission card is checked to verify the voter, and is disposed of, and the ballot card is collected, thus making the process anonymous. As of 2015, Skadian regions have introduced e-voting, allowing people to cast their votes or propose popular initiatives over the internet or through [[SMS|SMS messaging]].

In addition to voting for popular initiatives, citizens vote for the three levels of administrative division in Skade: the 153-member Althing, the 17 cantons, and the 2,941 municipal assembly seats. All three levels use the same voting system of proportional representation, but the Althing is the only level where earlier elections can be called. The current [[Election threshold|election threshold]] in Skade is 2%, as established by electoral reforms following the election of the Adelsköld government. Despite this relatively low threshold, only five parties managed to gain representation in the 2012 elections.

=== Administrative divisions ===
{{Main|Cantons of Skade}}

Skade is divided into '''[[Cantons of Skade|18 cantons]]''', known as {{lang|sk|kantonen}} ({{script|Runr|ᚴᚪᚿᛐᚬᚿᛂᚿ}}). The cantons are semi-autonomous, with their own elected parliaments and premiers, who represent their canton in the [[National Council (Skade)|National Council]]. Each canton is further divided into [[Counties of Skade|counties]] ({{lang|sk|'''fylker'''}}; {{script|Runr|ᚠᚤᛚᚴᛂᚱ}}) and from there into [[Municipalities of Skade|municipalities]], and are collectively known as {{lang|sk|'''småregjeringer'''}} or "little governments". The amount of cantons reached its current number of 17 with the separation of [[Kvällhälla canton|Kvällhälla]] from [[Frystmark canton|Frystmark]] in 1994.

The Gotalandic-speaking area of [[Brumundberg region|Brumundberg]] is actually a special administrative region, and is completely demilitarized in line with provisions derived from the [[Brumundberg referendum|2013 Brumundberg referendum]], and thus is considered a ''half-canton'' ({{lang|sk|halv-kantonen|link=}}). [[Arnarhvall]] is not actually a canton, but an [[Unincorporated territory|unincorporated territory]] of Skade, and maintains its status as a demilitarized free economic zone.

